WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:03.600
- [Narrator] The opening shot of El Camino

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.970
is not of Jesse Pinkman,
but of what he's looking at.

00:00:06.970 --> 00:00:10.970
When he does step into the
frame, he remains out of focus.

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:13.160
A shot like this puts the audience

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:15.260
into the character's perspective.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:18.320
We see the character we're
supposed to be connecting with;

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:22.620
but the focus isn't on them,
it's on their point of view.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:26.100
El Camino elevates Jesse
Pinkman from his position

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.820
as iconic supporting
character in Breaking Bad

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.240
to leading man.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:31.520
The approach to the world

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:34.410
of Breaking Bad in El Camino shifts from

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.380
a broader story that contains Jesse,

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:40.970
to Jesse's perspective as the story.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:43.780
In this video, I want to
examine the techniques

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.460
used by the filmmakers of El
Camino to show that shift.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.020
Placing us inside Jesse's perspective

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:51.043
throughout the film.

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.587
(serious music)

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:01.640
El Camino abandons the
sixteen by nine aspect ratio

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.300
common in TV, and used on Breaking Bad,

00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:09.300
for a 2.39:1 aspect ratio,
more common in cinema.

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:12.790
Director Vince Gilligan and
cinematographer Marshall Adams

00:01:12.790 --> 00:01:15.920
put this wider aspect
ratio to good use to place

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.480
Jesse front and center in the film.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.010
One of the ways they do
this is to use the edges

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:24.250
of the wider frame to keep Jesse in shots

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.830
even when he's not the main focus.

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:29.470
This keeps Jesse as a
constant visual presence

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:33.253
in many scenes, and the focus
for the audience overall.

00:01:34.090 --> 00:01:35.710
Let's look at this scene with Badger,

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:37.500
Skinny Pete, and Jesse.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:40.200
In scenes with the three
of them from Breaking Bad,

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:43.870
visually, they are all
treated with similar emphasis,

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:45.163
as you can see here.

00:01:47.087 --> 00:01:50.990
But in El Camino, Jesse
is the obvious emphasis

00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:54.300
in the frame, even when he's not speaking.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:56.120
In scenes like this one,
there's almost never

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:59.407
a shot without Jesse present in the frame.

00:01:59.407 --> 00:02:02.147
- A shower will make you
feel a whole lot better!

00:02:02.147 --> 00:02:05.020
Truth be told, you look
like you're hurting for one.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:07.280
- Church, no offense.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:08.600
- [Narrator] One of the interesting things

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:12.620
about El Camino is even though
the story centers on Jesse,

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.430
and he's the film's main character,

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:16.910
he doesn't do most of the talking.

00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:19.590
Much of the film is Jesse
listening and reacting

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:20.770
to other characters.

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:23.830
- Yo, I got Axe body spray,
I got Obsession for men,

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:25.310
I got Tommy Hilfinger.

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:27.180
Feel free to use any of
them you want, you know?

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.650
Use 'em all.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:29.930
- Obsession's the bomb, yo.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:32.040
I got it for him for Christmas.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.910
- [Narrator] So in many
scenes, even when other

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:36.760
characters are saying
important or pivotal lines,

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.910
the camera lingers on
Jesse and his reaction

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.850
as he's listening.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:44.870
- She brought it straight
in here to show me.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.550
She actually thought I
didn't know it was there.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.030
- [Narrator] But besides
the wider aspect ratio,

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:52.280
shallow depth of field is also used to

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.890
emphasize Jesse within the frame.

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:57.990
In many shots, Jesse is in
focus while the other characters

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:00.170
around him blur into the background.

00:03:00.170 --> 00:03:02.710
The composition and blocking
of characters in shots

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:05.050
also places focus on Jesse.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:06.120
Shots like this.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:07.120
- The news that bad?

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:10.770
- There's just a whole lot of it.

00:03:10.770 --> 00:03:15.410
- [Narrator] And this, frame
Jesse clearly at the center

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:17.750
of the image and the story.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:19.870
And even when he's not in the shot,

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:22.900
composition is used to
keep the focus on Jesse

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:24.680
in the audience's mind.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:27.860
In this shot of Todd, the
emphasis is actually on

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:30.510
Jesse's absence from the frame.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:33.380
Our attention is drawn to Jesse's absence

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:36.720
by a composition that
seems to be deliberately

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.240
cutting him out of the shot.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:41.600
When the shot pans over, revealing that he

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:42.820
wasn't cut out of the shot,

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:45.730
but not present in the front seat at all,

00:03:45.730 --> 00:03:49.080
the emphasis of the entire
moment becomes not Todd's

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:52.853
behavior, but Jesse's
experience of the situation.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:57.780
This shot uses the
actual shift in angle of

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:01.583
Jesse's point of view to
literally portray his perspective.

00:04:04.447 --> 00:04:07.390
Another example of using
blocking and composition

00:04:07.390 --> 00:04:10.310
to create perspective is
found in this scene from

00:04:10.310 --> 00:04:12.910
El Camino of Jesse in the cage.

00:04:12.910 --> 00:04:14.830
Throughout the scene,
the camera, and therefore

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:18.050
the viewers, stay inside
the cage with Jesse.

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:20.690
Only catching a glimpse
of Todd through the bars,

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:22.363
the same way Jesse does.

00:04:23.273 --> 00:04:25.300
(lighter clicking)

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.040
In similar scenes from Breaking Bad,

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:27.873
like you see here,

00:04:27.873 --> 00:04:31.350
the camera moves more freely
in and out of the cage,

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:34.210
establishing the scenes
from outside the cage.

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:36.110
Because in those scenes,
the emphasis isn't on

00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:38.470
Jesse's perspective as
a character as much as

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:41.790
the progression of all the
characters within the story.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:44.250
Notice the difference
in perspective in these

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:47.490
two shots of Todd lowering
items into the cage.

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:50.260
The shot from Breaking Bad
is from Todd's perspective,

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:53.970
and the shot in El Camino
is clearly from Jesse's.

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:57.780
In El Camino, the camera only
gets to be outside the cage

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:00.980
when Jesse himself is on the outside.

00:05:00.980 --> 00:05:03.010
We'll also notice, if
we look at the beginning

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:04.420
of this scene.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:05.253
- [Todd] Jesse?

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:07.440
- [Jesse] Yeah?

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:09.550
- [Narrator] How Todd
is introduced by voice

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:12.840
while the camera lingers
visually on Jesse.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:15.110
This places the emphasis
of the scene clearly

00:05:15.110 --> 00:05:17.063
on Jesse, instead of Todd.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:21.393
- [Todd] Everything good?

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:24.150
- Yeah.

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:26.210
- [Narrator] We can also
see the same technique used

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:27.470
at the beginning of the film,

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:30.230
where Mike is introduced by his voice,

00:05:30.230 --> 00:05:32.793
while Jesse's face is on screen.

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:36.090
- [Jesse] You know he's
not gonna be happy.

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.543
- [Mike] No, I suspect he won't.

00:05:40.030 --> 00:05:41.680
- [Narrator] Long takes, like this one

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:43.450
are also used in the movie

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:46.090
to place us in Jesse's perspective.

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:48.270
By staying with a
character without cutting

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:52.120
over a long period of time,
we experience the moment

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:53.763
in an unbroken manner.

00:05:56.066 --> 00:06:01.066
Close to what the character
experiences in real time.

00:06:01.391 --> 00:06:03.930
(suspenseful music)

00:06:03.930 --> 00:06:06.340
In this shot, the sound design and score

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:09.750
also play a role in
establishing the perspective.

00:06:09.750 --> 00:06:12.640
We hear what sounds
like Jesse's heartbeats

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:13.873
as tension mounts.

00:06:14.738 --> 00:06:17.738
(suspenseful music)

00:06:20.732 --> 00:06:22.723
- [Man] Yeah, yeah.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.567
And after that just turn off Broadway.

00:06:27.567 --> 00:06:30.770
- [Narrator] Narratively, the
use of editing and flashbacks

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:33.040
is also important to crafting the overall

00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:34.450
perspective of the film.

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:36.457
Quick cross-cuts, like this one,

00:06:41.540 --> 00:06:42.885
and this one,

00:06:42.885 --> 00:06:45.052
(yelling)

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:50.340
to moments that Jesse is remembering

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:53.910
essentially place us inside Jesse's mind.

00:06:53.910 --> 00:06:58.280
We're seeing what he's
visualizing as a memory.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:01.250
The longer flashbacks also
reflect this approach.

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:04.460
Each flashback is a
specific memory of Jesse's,

00:07:04.460 --> 00:07:07.730
and we see them through
his eyes, so to speak.

00:07:07.730 --> 00:07:10.290
These memories are triggered by what he's

00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:13.260
experiencing in the present narrative.

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:15.750
In this way, the flashbacks
don't just act as a tool

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:19.000
to drive the plot forward,
but a tool to place us deeper

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:21.903
inside Jesse's mind and his experience.

00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:26.720
All of these techniques working together

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:29.790
put the audience inside
the mind and experience

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:31.340
of Jesse Pinkman.

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:34.380
And it's an important part
of what makes this film work.

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:37.300
With this shift in perspective, El Camino

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:40.910
becomes a fresh view into
the world of Breaking Bad

00:07:40.910 --> 00:07:43.040
and the character of Jesse Pinkman.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:46.320
We get a closer look at
the supporting character

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:49.770
who grew into a central part of what made

00:07:49.770 --> 00:07:51.663
Breaking Bad so great.

